        "at": "2026-11-08T22:38:26"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "act": "BLUF",
      "narration_text": "Your phone never sends anything to a GPS satellite. It only listens. It times how long each signal takes to arrive, and works out the one place you could be.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "speed": 0.95,
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "GpsKeyPoints",
          "props": {
            "points": [
              {
                "text": "never sends",
                "at": 0.024
              },
              {
                "text": "only listens",
                "at": 0.3318,
                "accent": true
              },
              {
                "text": "times the signals → finds you",
                "at": 0.4645
              }
            ],
            "durationSeconds": 10.85
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 10.45,
      "render_duration_s": 10.458333333333334,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-11-08T22:38:26"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "act": "PROBLEM",
      "narration_text": "Start with the problem. Your phone has no idea where it is, and no way to ask. All it can do is listen to radio signals from about thirty satellites, twenty thousand kilometers up.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "speed": 0.95,
      "estimated_duration_s": 15,
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "GpsSky",
          "props": {
            "altitudeLabel": "~20,000 km up",
            "count": 30,
            "phoneAt": 0.1794,
            "satsAt": 0.6656,
            "altAt": 0.7602,
            "durationSeconds": 12.26
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 11.86,
      "render_duration_s": 11.875,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-11-08T22:38:26"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "MECHANISM",
      "narration_text": "Each satellite broadcasts two things, over and over: where it is, and exactly what time it is, from an atomic clock on board. Your phone notes when each signal arrives. Multiply that delay by the speed of light, and you get the distance. Seven hundredths of a second means about twenty-one thousand kilometers.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "speed": 0.95,
      "estimated_duration_s": 20,
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "GpsTimeDistance",
          "props": {
            "where": "I'm here: (x, y, z)",
            "when": "It's exactly 12:00:00.000000",
            "travelS": 0.07059,
            "rangeKm": 21162,
            "broadcastAt": 0.0168,
            "clockAt": 0.3198,
            "arriveAt": 0.4396,
            "mathAt": 0.5663,
            "resultAt": 0.7832,
            "durationSeconds": 20.2
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 19.8,
      "render_duration_s": 19.833333333333332,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-11-08T22:38:26"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "act": "FRAMEWORK",
      "narration_text": "One distance tells you you're somewhere on a giant sphere around that satellite. A second sphere narrows it to a circle. A third narrows it to just two points, and one of them is out in space. Here it's flattened into circles, but the idea is the same: you're where they all meet.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "speed": 0.95,
      "estimated_duration_s": 22,
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "GpsCircles",
          "props": {
            "c1At": 0.0,
            "c2At": 0.2475,
            "c3At": 0.398,
            "captionAt": 0.7023,
            "meetAt": 0.9052,
            "caption": "Flattened to circles. In 3D they're spheres.",
            "durationSeconds": 17.94
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 17.54,
      "render_duration_s": 17.541666666666668,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B04.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-11-08T22:38:26"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "act": "CATCH",
      "narration_text": "But there's a catch. Your phone's clock is cheap. If it's off by just one millionth of a second, every distance is off by three hundred meters. So your phone uses a fourth satellite to work out its own clock error. Three for where, one for when.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "speed": 0.95,
      "estimated_duration_s": 16,
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "GpsClockError",
          "props": {
            "errorLabel": "phone clock off by 0.000001 s",
            "meters": 300,
            "clockAt": 0.109,
            "swellAt": 0.3663,
            "fourthAt": 0.6451,
            "whereAt": 0.8466,
            "whenAt": 0.9113,
            "durationSeconds": 15.78
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 15.38,
      "render_duration_s": 15.416666666666666,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B05.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-11-08T22:38:26"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B06",
      "act": "WORKED EXAMPLE",
      "narration_text": "Here it is on real numbers. I gave a solver four satellites above Boston Common and a phone clock that's off by one millionth of a second. Ignore the clock, and the answer lands three hundred ninety meters away. Solve for it, and it lands right on the spot, and finds the clock error too.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "speed": 0.95,
      "estimated_duration_s": 19,
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "GpsDemo",
          "props": {
            "place": "Boston Common",
            "ranges": [
              21162,
              21343,
              21268,
              21854
            ],
            "travelMs": [
              70.59,
              71.19,
              70.94,
              72.9
            ],
            "clockUs": 1.0,
            "ignoreM": 390.2,
            "solvedUs": 1.0,
            "setupAt": 0.1471,
            "ignoreAt": 0.4614,
            "solveAt": 0.7058,
            "foundAt": 0.8762,
            "footnote": "Satellite positions chosen for illustration; the solver and physics are real (demo/gps_demo.py).",
            "footnoteShort": "Real solver, example sky.",
            "durationSeconds": 18.9
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B06.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 18.5,
      "render_duration_s": 18.5,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B06.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-11-08T22:38:26"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B07",
      "act": "SURPRISE",
      "narration_text": "One more surprise. Because of Einstein's relativity, the satellite clocks run fast by about thirty-eight millionths of a second a day. Left alone, your position would drift about eleven kilometers every day. So the clocks are set to tick slightly slow before launch.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "speed": 0.95,
      "estimated_duration_s": 17,
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "GpsRelativity",
          "props": {
            "net": 38,
            "gravity": 45,
            "speed": 7,
            "driftKm": 11,
            "clocksAt": 0.2066,
            "whyAt": 0.1453,
            "driftAt": 0.496,
            "fixAt": 0.8207,
            "durationSeconds": 17.62
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B07.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 17.22,
      "render_duration_s": 17.25,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B07.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-11-08T22:38:26"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B08",
      "act": "WHEN NOT TO",
      "narration_text": "So when can you trust it? Outdoors under open sky, a phone is usually accurate to within about five meters. Between tall buildings, signals bounce, and your dot can jump across the street. Indoors and underground, the signal barely gets through, so your phone guesses from Wi-Fi and cell towers. And remember, GPS only listens. The satellites never know where you are, but your apps might.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "speed": 0.95,
      "estimated_duration_s": 24,
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "RagWhenToUse",
          "props": {
            "useHeader": "Trust it",
            "skipHeader": "Expect it to slip",
            "useHeaderAt": 0.0032,
            "skipHeaderAt": 0.2721,
            "useItems": [
              {
                "text": "outdoors, open sky",
                "at": 0.0584
              },
              {
                "text": "within about 5 m",
                "at": 0.1853
              }
            ],
            "skipItems": [
              {
                "text": "between tall buildings",
                "at": 0.2721
              },
              {
                "text": "indoors: Wi-Fi and cell towers",
                "at": 0.4653
              }
            ],
            "warning": "GPS only listens. Your apps might share.",
            "warningAt": 0.78,
            "durationSeconds": 25.36
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B08.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 24.96,
      "render_duration_s": 25.0,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B08.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-11-08T22:38:26"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B09",
      "act": "VERDICT",
      "narration_text": "Three things to keep. GPS turns time into distance. Three satellites for where, one for when. And GPS only listens; your apps are what share your location.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "speed": 0.95,
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "shot": {
